<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gethsemane</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Garden of Gethsemane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gethsemane is a garden at the foot of the Mount of Olives in East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> where, according to the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> underwent the Agony in the Garden and was arrested before his crucifixion. The name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> words meaning &quot;oil press&quot;. Four sites near the western foot of the Mount of Olives are claimed by different denominations, among them the garden of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> of All Nations and the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> orchard; modern scholarship acknowledges that the exact location is unknown. It became a focus for early Christian pilgrims, and the Pilgrim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> visited it in 333.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gethsemane.jpg" alt="Gethsemane" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mewasul · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Getsemane2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
